Docker is a containerization platform used to build, package, and run applications in isolated environments called containers.
Containers are lightweight because they share the host operating system kernel, unlike virtual machines.
Docker ensures consistency by allowing applications to run the same way across development, testing, and production environments.
Docker Images are read-only templates used to create containers.
Docker Containers are running instances of Docker images.
Dockerfile is a text file that contains instructions to build a Docker image.
Docker Hub is a public registry where Docker images can be stored, shared, and downloaded.
